15.29, "analyst_adjustment": -0.49, "key_factors": ["Davids holds a roughly 95-to-1 fundraising advantage over Jenkins ($4.5M raised vs $47K)", "All ten expert ratings place the seat as Solid or Safe Democratic", "Incumbent Davids runs in a D+2 district with a favorable environment", "Polymarket prices Davids near 95% to win"], "rationale": "Davids' overwhelming money advantage, incumbency in a slightly Democratic-leaning district, and unanimous Solid or Safe Democratic ratings from ten outlets leave Jenkins without a visible path, and markets price her win odds near 95 percent.", "watch": "A late wave of national Republican spending or a sharp shift in the political environment could put the seat in play.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Sharice Davids (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Sharice Davids seeks re-election in Kansas' 3rd District, a suburban Kansas City seat that leans slightly Democratic. She cruised through the August primary and faces Eric Jenkins, a Republican who has raised roughly $47,000, along with Libertarian Steven Hohe. Davids has raised about $4.5 million and holds $1.8 million on hand, an enormous financial edge.\n\nEvery major race rating places the district in Solid or Safe Democratic territory, and prediction markets give Davids roughly a 95 percent chance of winning. No public general-election polling is available, but the combination of incumbency, the district's lean, and the money gap leaves Jenkins a narrow path. Davids has campaigned on healthcare costs and rural hospital funding, while the race has stayed low-key, with a local forum canceled after candidates declined to participate.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9826, "margin": 15.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.979, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2